---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

/**
 * The privacy page.
 *
 * Written from what the site actually loads rather than from a template. The
 * external hosts were read out of the built HTML — `googletagmanager.com` and
 * the self-hosted Umami, and nothing else; the fonts are the five `.woff2`
 * files served from this domain, so there is no Google Fonts request to
 * disclose.
 *
 * It says plainly that there is no consent banner. That is a real decision
 * with real implications in some jurisdictions, and a privacy page that
 * quietly omits it is worse than one that states it. Nothing here is legal
 * advice and it deliberately claims no compliance with any particular regime.
 */
const UPDATED = "31 August 2026";
---

<Base
  title="Privacy"
  description="What this website collects, what the Flume application does not, and what BitTorrent itself exposes."
>
  <section class="wrap head">
    <p class="eyebrow">Privacy</p>
    <h1 class="head__title">What this site collects.</h1>
    <p class="lead head__lead">
      Short version: two analytics tools on the website, nothing in the
      application, and no way to send us anything about yourself even if you
      wanted to.
    </p>
    <p class="updated">Last updated {UPDATED}</p>
  </section>

  <div class="wrap prose-page">
    <section class="block">
      <h2>The website</h2>
      <p>
        Two analytics tools run on these pages, and they are the only things
        here that load from anywhere other than this domain.
      </p>

      <div class="tools">
        <div class="tool">
          <h3>Google Analytics 4</h3>
          <p>
            Property <span class="num">G-KNGWP21KK5</span>. It <strong
              >sets cookies</strong
            > and sends page views, along with the usual coarse details — approximate
            location from your IP address, browser, device type, and the page you
            came from — to Google, where Google's own{" "}
            <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" rel="noopener"
              >privacy policy</a
            > applies.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="tool">
          <h3>Umami, self-hosted</h3>
          <p>
            Runs on a server we control. It is <strong>cookieless</strong> and records
            page views without building a profile that follows you between sites.
            Nothing it collects is shared with anyone.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        Both are there on purpose rather than by accident. Google Analytics is
        blocked by a large share of the people who read a page like this, so
        running a self-hosted tool alongside it is the only way to know how much
        the first one is missing. The gap between the two numbers is the
        measurement.
      </p>

      <p class="note">
        <strong>There is no cookie banner.</strong> Google Analytics sets cookies
        and you are not asked first. That is worth stating rather than burying: if
        you would rather not be counted, any content blocker stops it, and Firefox
        and Safari block it by default.
      </p>

      <h3>What is not here</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>No forms, accounts, or newsletter.</strong> There is nothing on
          this site you can submit, so there is nothing for us to store.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>No third-party fonts.</strong> The five font files are served from
          this domain, so no request goes to Google Fonts or any other host.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>No advertising, and nothing sold or shared.</strong>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        The site is hosted on Sevalla and served through Cloudflare, both of
        which keep ordinary server logs of requests. Downloads are served by
        GitHub, so following a download link is a request to GitHub and their
        policy covers it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block">
      <h2>The application</h2>
      <p>
        Flume itself sends us nothing. There is no analytics library in it, no
        automatic update check, and no account — verified in the source, which
        is <a href="https://github.com/adamgreenwell/flume" rel="noopener"
          >public</a
        > if you would rather check than take our word for it.
      </p>
      <p>
        It is not silent on the network, because a torrent client cannot be. It
        talks to trackers, to peers, and to the distributed hash table. It does
        not talk to us.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block">
      <h2>What BitTorrent exposes</h2>
      <p>
        This part is not about Flume, and no client can change it. It is worth
        saying anyway.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Peers can see your IP address.</strong> That is how the protocol
          works — peers connect to each other directly. Anyone in a swarm can record
          who else is in it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>DHT announces that you are in a swarm.</strong> You can turn it
          off in Settings; magnet links stop working when you do.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong
            >Flume can route peer connections through a SOCKS5 proxy</strong
          >, which changes what peers see. It routes peer traffic and is not a
          general-purpose anonymity tool. If anonymity is your threat model, a
          torrent client is not the control you are looking for.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="block">
      <h2>Questions</h2>
      <p>
        Email <a href="mailto:adamgreenwell@gmail.com"
          >adamgreenwell@gmail.com</a
        >, or open an issue on{" "}
        <a href="https://github.com/adamgreenwell/flume-site" rel="noopener"
          >the repository for this site</a
        >. If something on this page is wrong or out of date, that is a bug and
        it will be fixed.
      </p>
      <p class="note">
        This page describes what happens. It is not legal advice and it does not
        claim compliance with any particular regulation.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .head {
    padding-top: clamp(2.5rem, 1rem + 5vw, 4.5rem);
  }

  .head__title {
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
    font-size: var(--t-display);
  }

  .head__lead {
    margin-top: 1rem;
    max-width: 60ch;
  }

  .updated {
    margin-top: 1rem;
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    color: var(--fg-3);
  }

  /* The measure goes on the sections, not on the `.wrap` element. Narrowing
     the wrap itself leaves its `margin-inline: auto` centring the result, which
     indented the whole body away from the heading above it. */
  .prose-page {
    display: grid;
    gap: clamp(2.5rem, 1.5rem + 3vw, 3.5rem);
    margin-top: clamp(2.5rem, 1.5rem + 3vw, 3.5rem);
  }

  .block {
    max-width: 78ch;
  }

  .block h2 {
    padding-bottom: 0.9rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    font-size: var(--t-title);
  }

  .block h3 {
    margin-top: 1.75rem;
    font-size: 1.02rem;
    letter-spacing: -0.014em;
  }

  .block p {
    margin-top: 1rem;
    color: var(--fg-1);
  }

  .block ul {
    margin-top: 1rem;
    padding-left: 1.35rem;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.6rem;
    color: var(--fg-1);
  }

  .block li::marker {
    color: var(--fg-3);
  }

  .block strong {
    color: var(--fg-0);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .block a {
    color: var(--acc);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 2px;
  }

  .tools {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 0.75rem;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .tool {
    padding: 1.25rem 1.3rem 1.4rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--r-lg);
    background: var(--bg-1);
  }

  .tool h3 {
    margin-top: 0;
    font-size: 1rem;
  }

  .tool p {
    font-size: var(--t-small);
    color: var(--fg-2);
  }

  .note {
    margin-top: 1.5rem !important;
    padding: 0.9rem 1.1rem;
    border: 1px solid color-mix(in oklab, var(--warn) 35%, transparent);
    border-radius: var(--r-lg);
    background: color-mix(in oklab, var(--warn) 8%, transparent);
    font-size: var(--t-small);
    color: var(--fg-1) !important;
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .tools {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }
  }
</style>
